1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,350
It's Monday, it isn't even noon yet,

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:05,238
and Daniel has already said yes eleven times.

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:06,550
It started early,

4
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,938
with a report Richard asked for in passing,

5
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:10,512
and then Margaret's spreadsheet.

6
00:00:10,725 --> 00:00:13,300
After that came covering a meeting that wasn't his,

7
00:00:13,488 --> 00:00:16,588
ordering a birthday cake and staying late on Thursday.

8
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:17,825
And to top it off,

9
00:00:17,862 --> 00:00:20,212
he agreed to a weekend shift he really didn't

10
00:00:20,212 --> 00:00:22,112
want. So at seven p.m.,

11
00:00:22,175 --> 00:00:23,988
when the office is almost empty,

12
00:00:24,150 --> 00:00:25,862
Daniel is still at his desk,

13
00:00:25,925 --> 00:00:28,225
doing everyone's work except his own.

14
00:00:28,438 --> 00:00:31,050
If you've ever said yes while everything inside you

15
00:00:31,050 --> 00:00:31,900
was screaming no,

16
00:00:32,125 --> 00:00:33,412
this video is for you.

17
00:00:33,638 --> 00:00:36,188
Because that yes comes from a very old voice

18
00:00:36,188 --> 00:00:37,575
that, believe it or not,

19
00:00:37,775 --> 00:00:39,625
is only trying to keep you safe.

20
00:00:40,242 --> 00:00:41,167
To understand it,

21
00:00:41,254 --> 00:00:42,367
you need to meet Daniel.

22
00:00:42,592 --> 00:00:44,430
He joined the company four months ago,

23
00:00:44,480 --> 00:00:47,067
and everyone agrees he's the nicest person on the

24
00:00:47,067 --> 00:00:50,617
floor. His email signature literally says he's always happy

25
00:00:50,617 --> 00:00:53,092
to help. He laughs at every joke,

26
00:00:53,267 --> 00:00:54,942
agrees in every meeting,

27
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:57,055
and he has never said no to anyone.

28
00:00:57,267 --> 00:00:59,167
Of course, people love him for it.

29
00:00:59,392 --> 00:01:00,805
The problem is that lately,

30
00:01:00,967 --> 00:01:03,255
Daniel doesn't love his own life very much.

31
00:01:03,492 --> 00:01:04,555
He isn't faking it;

32
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:06,254
he genuinely likes helping.

33
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:08,792
But he can no longer tell where helping ends

34
00:01:08,804 --> 00:01:10,504
and where he starts to disappear.

35
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:13,333
Tuesday's meeting makes it even clearer.

36
00:01:13,545 --> 00:01:16,170
Richard proposes skipping the testing phase,

37
00:01:16,170 --> 00:01:18,358
the step where you check that a product works

38
00:01:18,358 --> 00:01:19,733
before customers touch it,

39
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:20,908
to launch faster.

40
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:22,708
Daniel knows it's a mistake,

41
00:01:22,758 --> 00:01:25,195
because the numbers are open on his laptop and

42
00:01:25,195 --> 00:01:25,983
none of them add up.

43
00:01:26,195 --> 00:01:27,358
Then Richard asks:

44
00:01:27,583 --> 00:01:30,120
"Everyone agree?" Someone raises a hand,

45
00:01:30,295 --> 00:01:31,333
then someone else,

46
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:34,045
and within seconds the whole room seems to have

47
00:01:34,045 --> 00:01:35,995
decided. Daniel looks around,

48
00:01:36,170 --> 00:01:38,070
feels the silence getting heavier,

49
00:01:38,245 --> 00:01:40,383
and slowly raises his hand too.

50
00:01:40,608 --> 00:01:43,270
That evening, replaying the meeting in his head,

51
00:01:43,458 --> 00:01:45,845
he can't even explain to himself why he did

52
00:01:45,845 --> 00:01:47,570
it. And here's the strange part:

53
00:01:47,858 --> 00:01:49,808
he wasn't the only one with doubts.

54
00:01:50,033 --> 00:01:51,358
Margaret thought the same,

55
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:53,508
but nobody wanted to be the first to break

56
00:01:53,508 --> 00:01:56,345
the agreement, so everyone voted for something they didn't

57
00:01:56,345 --> 00:01:58,951
believe in. Then Saturday arrives.

58
00:01:59,176 --> 00:02:01,164
His sister calls him three times,

59
00:02:01,176 --> 00:02:02,314
and he doesn't pick up,

60
00:02:02,326 --> 00:02:04,876
because he's still finishing Margaret's spreadsheet.

61
00:02:05,101 --> 00:02:06,951
So she sends a message instead,

62
00:02:07,126 --> 00:02:08,026
and it stings:

63
00:02:08,276 --> 00:02:10,314
he never has time for anything anymore.

64
00:02:10,551 --> 00:02:12,126
He types back "sorry,

65
00:02:12,326 --> 00:02:14,864
work", puts the phone down and stares at the

66
00:02:14,864 --> 00:02:16,564
ceiling. Without noticing,

67
00:02:16,576 --> 00:02:19,414
being liked by everyone had turned into a full-time

68
00:02:19,414 --> 00:02:21,188
job. And on Sunday night,

69
00:02:21,314 --> 00:02:23,864
that familiar knot shows up in his stomach,

70
00:02:23,926 --> 00:02:26,564
because another week full of yeses is about to

71
00:02:26,564 --> 00:02:29,337
begin. So let's put a face to that yes.

72
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:30,562
Meet The Follower,

73
00:02:30,712 --> 00:02:33,150
the voice that goes wherever the group goes.

74
00:02:33,362 --> 00:02:34,887
It's the voice that whispers:

75
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:36,874
"Just agree. Don't make waves.

76
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:38,324
If everyone's doing it,

77
00:02:38,387 --> 00:02:42,187
it must be right." And in 1951 a psychologist

78
00:02:42,187 --> 00:02:44,874
named Solomon Asch decided to put that voice to

79
00:02:44,874 --> 00:02:47,424
the test. The task looked almost too easy.

80
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:49,400
One card showed a single line,

81
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,512
and another card showed three lines of different lengths.

82
00:02:52,737 --> 00:02:54,650
People just had to say which of the three

83
00:02:54,650 --> 00:02:56,112
was as long as the first one,

84
00:02:56,287 --> 00:02:57,987
something any child could do.

85
00:02:58,622 --> 00:02:59,698
But there was a catch.

86
00:02:59,910 --> 00:03:01,972
Everyone else in the room was an actor,

87
00:03:02,047 --> 00:03:03,172
and one by one,

88
00:03:03,435 --> 00:03:06,285
out loud, they all gave the same wrong answer

89
00:03:06,285 --> 00:03:09,122
on purpose. Only then was it the real volunteer's

90
00:03:09,122 --> 00:03:11,797
turn. The result surprised even Asch:

91
00:03:11,972 --> 00:03:14,222
about three out of four people went along with

92
00:03:14,222 --> 00:03:15,635
the group at least once,

93
00:03:15,810 --> 00:03:17,710
even with the right answer in front of their

94
00:03:17,710 --> 00:03:20,698
eyes. Later, many of them admitted they knew the

95
00:03:20,698 --> 00:03:21,460
group was wrong.

96
00:03:21,685 --> 00:03:23,647
They simply didn't want to be the only one

97
00:03:23,647 --> 00:03:24,760
saying something different.

98
00:03:24,972 --> 00:03:26,497
And think about what that means.

99
00:03:26,710 --> 00:03:29,210
Nobody forced them and there was nothing to win.

100
00:03:29,422 --> 00:03:32,035
The discomfort of standing alone was enough to make

101
00:03:32,035 --> 00:03:33,347
them doubt their own eyes.

102
00:03:33,976 --> 00:03:36,951
Now, The Follower doesn't visit everyone equally.

103
00:03:37,163 --> 00:03:40,288
It especially loves people who just arrived somewhere new,

104
00:03:40,526 --> 00:03:42,826
people who grew up keeping the peace at home,

105
00:03:43,026 --> 00:03:45,513
and anyone who measures their worth by how useful

106
00:03:45,513 --> 00:03:47,388
they are. So how can you tell it's with

107
00:03:47,388 --> 00:03:49,351
you? There are four signs that show up a

108
00:03:49,351 --> 00:03:52,163
lot. You say yes before you've even thought about

109
00:03:52,163 --> 00:03:54,538
it. You wait to hear what others think before

110
00:03:54,538 --> 00:03:55,388
giving your opinion.

111
00:03:55,601 --> 00:03:57,888
You apologize for things that aren't your fault.

112
00:03:58,101 --> 00:03:59,901
And when you finally do say no,

113
00:04:00,063 --> 00:04:01,851
the guilt follows you for days.

114
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:04,878
Here's what The Follower does to Daniel's month.

115
00:04:05,153 --> 00:04:07,553
First, his own project falls behind,

116
00:04:07,653 --> 00:04:09,890
because his time goes to everyone else's.

117
00:04:10,103 --> 00:04:11,803
Then the tiredness arrives,

118
00:04:11,816 --> 00:04:14,353
and after the tiredness comes a quiet anger.

119
00:04:14,578 --> 00:04:15,790
And it isn't aimed at them,

120
00:04:15,866 --> 00:04:16,903
but at himself.

121
00:04:17,116 --> 00:04:18,728
And that's where the trap closes.

122
00:04:18,953 --> 00:04:20,465
The more he says yes,

123
00:04:20,503 --> 00:04:22,366
the more people ask him for things,

124
00:04:22,378 --> 00:04:25,140
so every new no becomes harder than the last.

125
00:04:25,353 --> 00:04:26,853
And the team gets used to it:

126
00:04:27,166 --> 00:04:29,990
Daniel becomes the one who always says yes,

127
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:32,678
so the extra work keeps landing on his desk.

128
00:04:33,308 --> 00:04:34,745
Remember Richard's launch,

129
00:04:34,758 --> 00:04:36,120
the one without testing?

130
00:04:36,333 --> 00:04:37,820
It goes live on Monday,

131
00:04:37,895 --> 00:04:39,820
and by the end of the day customers have

132
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:41,333
reported two hundred errors.

133
00:04:41,545 --> 00:04:42,583
Daniel saw it coming.

134
00:04:42,808 --> 00:04:43,795
He had the numbers.

135
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:45,883
He just didn't want to be the only hand

136
00:04:45,883 --> 00:04:46,670
that stayed down.

137
00:04:46,895 --> 00:04:48,983
His silence didn't protect anyone.

138
00:04:49,196 --> 00:04:51,283
It only hid the problem until it was too

139
00:04:51,283 --> 00:04:52,320
big to ignore.

140
00:04:52,533 --> 00:04:54,595
And this happens in a lot of teams:

141
00:04:54,958 --> 00:04:57,545
often, the first person to spot a problem is

142
00:04:57,545 --> 00:04:59,208
the newest or the quietest,

143
00:04:59,246 --> 00:05:01,570
the one who feels least allowed to say it.

144
00:05:02,206 --> 00:05:03,369
But here's the twist:

145
00:05:03,432 --> 00:05:05,044
The Follower is not your enemy.

146
00:05:05,306 --> 00:05:06,506
For our ancestors,

147
00:05:06,556 --> 00:05:08,131
it worked more like a bodyguard.

148
00:05:08,356 --> 00:05:11,069
Picture a small group crossing the savanna when,

149
00:05:11,119 --> 00:05:13,244
suddenly, everyone starts running.

150
00:05:13,456 --> 00:05:14,719
Would you stop to ask why?

151
00:05:14,944 --> 00:05:17,332
The ones who stopped to think often didn't get

152
00:05:17,332 --> 00:05:18,269
a second chance;

153
00:05:18,382 --> 00:05:20,532
the ones who ran with the group survived.

154
00:05:20,744 --> 00:05:21,744
In other words,

155
00:05:21,782 --> 00:05:24,344
copying the group was a fast and cheap way

156
00:05:24,344 --> 00:05:25,182
to stay safe,

157
00:05:25,219 --> 00:05:27,732
because the group usually knew something you didn't.

158
00:05:27,944 --> 00:05:30,369
And standing apart could cost you your place in

159
00:05:30,369 --> 00:05:31,482
it, and with it,

160
00:05:31,719 --> 00:05:34,419
your protection. That's why part of your brain still

161
00:05:34,419 --> 00:05:36,282
treats being left out as a threat.

162
00:05:36,506 --> 00:05:40,319
Brain studies even suggest that social rejection activates

163
00:05:40,319 --> 00:05:42,769
some of the same areas as physical pain.

164
00:05:43,404 --> 00:05:46,342
Today, though, the danger is rarely a lion.

165
00:05:46,554 --> 00:05:47,517
It's a spreadsheet,

166
00:05:47,604 --> 00:05:49,792
a meeting, or a message on Slack,

167
00:05:49,817 --> 00:05:51,879
the chat app many offices use.

168
00:05:52,104 --> 00:05:54,230
The problem is that your body can't tell the

169
00:05:54,230 --> 00:05:57,317
difference. So disagreeing in a meeting can make your

170
00:05:57,317 --> 00:05:59,642
heart race, as if the group were about to

171
00:05:59,642 --> 00:06:00,530
leave you behind.

172
00:06:00,742 --> 00:06:02,504
So the bodyguard isn't broken.

173
00:06:02,717 --> 00:06:04,954
It's just protecting you from the wrong things.

174
00:06:05,167 --> 00:06:07,679
And that explains why saying no can feel so

175
00:06:07,679 --> 00:06:10,217
dramatic, even when the request is tiny.

176
00:06:10,838 --> 00:06:11,863
A few days later,

177
00:06:12,050 --> 00:06:15,388
Elena notices the dark circles under Daniel's eyes and

178
00:06:15,388 --> 00:06:16,600
invites him for a coffee.

179
00:06:16,838 --> 00:06:18,200
He admits he's exhausted.

180
00:06:18,426 --> 00:06:20,076
But instead of giving him advice,

181
00:06:20,226 --> 00:06:21,600
she asks him a question.

182
00:06:21,888 --> 00:06:23,275
"If nobody would get upset,

183
00:06:23,463 --> 00:06:25,976
what would you have answered this week?" At first

184
00:06:25,976 --> 00:06:28,525
Daniel laughs, but then he really thinks about it,

185
00:06:28,613 --> 00:06:29,738
and the list is long:

186
00:06:30,088 --> 00:06:31,413
no to the weekend shift,

187
00:06:31,488 --> 00:06:32,488
no to the cake,

188
00:06:32,588 --> 00:06:33,463
and above all,

189
00:06:33,663 --> 00:06:34,838
no to that launch.

190
00:06:35,063 --> 00:06:37,050
Then Elena tells him something surprising.

191
00:06:37,263 --> 00:06:38,738
In Asch's experiment,

192
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:41,000
when just one other person in the room gave

193
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:41,788
the right answer,

194
00:06:41,938 --> 00:06:44,226
far fewer people followed the wrong one.

195
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:46,488
One ally was enough to break the spell.

196
00:06:47,116 --> 00:06:48,166
And that's the update.

197
00:06:48,379 --> 00:06:50,428
It isn't about caring less about people,

198
00:06:50,591 --> 00:06:52,516
but about not disappearing for them.

199
00:06:52,741 --> 00:06:54,091
Here are four ways to do it.

200
00:06:54,354 --> 00:06:57,354
First, pause. Instead of the automatic yes,

201
00:06:57,404 --> 00:06:59,216
say you'll check and get back to them,

202
00:06:59,266 --> 00:07:01,854
because ten minutes is usually enough for the voice

203
00:07:01,854 --> 00:07:02,554
to calm down.

204
00:07:02,778 --> 00:07:04,416
Second, count the cost.

205
00:07:04,629 --> 00:07:07,028
Every yes is also a no to something else,

206
00:07:07,041 --> 00:07:08,178
so ask yourself:

207
00:07:08,441 --> 00:07:09,891
if I say yes to this,

208
00:07:09,904 --> 00:07:10,941
what am I giving up?

209
00:07:11,191 --> 00:07:13,304
Third, say no to the task,

210
00:07:13,354 --> 00:07:14,304
not to the person.

211
00:07:14,566 --> 00:07:17,204
For example: "I can't take this one this week,

212
00:07:17,278 --> 00:07:19,241
but I can help you find someone who can."

213
00:07:19,578 --> 00:07:21,216
And fourth, be the one,

214
00:07:21,416 --> 00:07:22,341
or find the one.

215
00:07:22,566 --> 00:07:23,741
If you see a problem,

216
00:07:23,879 --> 00:07:26,391
say it, and if speaking up alone feels too

217
00:07:26,391 --> 00:07:28,778
hard, find one ally before the meeting.

218
00:07:28,991 --> 00:07:29,954
So try it right now.

219
00:07:30,191 --> 00:07:32,304
Think of one yes you gave this week that

220
00:07:32,304 --> 00:07:33,291
you didn't really mean.

221
00:07:33,504 --> 00:07:35,328
What would a kind no have sounded like?

222
00:07:35,541 --> 00:07:38,091
And remember, the first no is the hardest,

223
00:07:38,091 --> 00:07:40,516
but each one after that gets a little easier.

224
00:07:41,130 --> 00:07:42,093
At the next meeting,

225
00:07:42,155 --> 00:07:44,080
Richard asks his usual question:

226
00:07:44,355 --> 00:07:46,268
"Everyone agree?" This time,

227
00:07:46,455 --> 00:07:47,680
Daniel takes a breath.

228
00:07:47,968 --> 00:07:50,230
"Not yet. There are some numbers you should see

229
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:51,968
first." And right away,

230
00:07:51,968 --> 00:07:54,880
Sarah nods: "I'd like to see them too." The

231
00:07:54,880 --> 00:07:55,780
room goes quiet,

232
00:07:55,992 --> 00:07:57,230
until Richard says:

233
00:07:57,530 --> 00:08:00,593
"Okay. Show me." The Follower is still there,

234
00:08:00,668 --> 00:08:02,530
but now it walks beside Daniel,

235
00:08:02,705 --> 00:08:03,680
not in front of him.

236
00:08:03,893 --> 00:08:05,042
So, a quick recap.

237
00:08:05,268 --> 00:08:07,818
The Follower makes you say yes and go along

238
00:08:07,818 --> 00:08:08,330
with the group.

239
00:08:08,542 --> 00:08:09,893
It exists because,

240
00:08:09,893 --> 00:08:11,143
for our ancestors,

241
00:08:11,143 --> 00:08:12,630
the group meant survival.

242
00:08:12,855 --> 00:08:14,518
And you update it by pausing,

243
00:08:14,555 --> 00:08:15,680
counting the cost,

244
00:08:15,742 --> 00:08:18,268
saying no to the task and not the person,

245
00:08:18,368 --> 00:08:19,755
and finding one ally.

246
00:08:19,980 --> 00:08:22,530
Because being kind is not the same as saying

247
00:08:22,530 --> 00:08:23,418
yes to everything.

248
00:08:23,630 --> 00:08:26,305
Sometimes, the kindest thing you can give someone is

249
00:08:26,305 --> 00:08:27,168
an honest answer.

250
00:08:27,393 --> 00:08:28,705
So tell me in the comments:

251
00:08:28,868 --> 00:08:30,755
what's the hardest no you've ever said?

252
00:08:30,968 --> 00:08:33,905
And subscribe, because next time we're back with Sarah,

253
00:08:33,993 --> 00:08:35,955
and the voice that keeps telling her what she

254
00:08:35,955 --> 00:08:36,605
should be doing.
